{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith","id":1554198,"title":"Mage with True Faith","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:11:31-07:00","id":1554198,"title":"Mage with True Faith","content":"So, I'd love to hear some opinions regarding this question posed to me in one of my games. What do you think of the idea of a religious mage that also has True Faith besides his Magick?<br /> <br /> Edit: The story is in Sorcerer's Crusade, so not really modern setting (it's set in the 16th century)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-06T10:17:31-07:00","id":1554200,"title":"","content":"It's fully viable especially for Chorus and Dreamspeakers. It also fits Batini and Hermetics well. But Faith can fit a wide range of characters. <br /> <br /> Also, in our reality, theurgy (the use of magic for spiritual growth in the worship of God or gods) is a real practice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-06T13:26:19-07:00","id":1554206,"title":"","content":"In pretty much every edition, mages have had access to True Faith on top of their magic. So there's no fundamental metaphysical problem with it... or it wouldn't be a mechanical option in the game.<br /> <br /> Personally, I tend to advise against TF in my games - and Mage very much so - on more player focused grounds: it's almost always going to be a complete waste of your freebies/XP. TF is generally extreme game resource expensive for what it does, and you have actual magic to sling around. There are evocative Merits and Flaws (though not all of them are in the SC edition's books directly if that's an issue) that help emphasize a PC's faith in much more meaningful and tangible ways to the game than TF really accomplishes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-07T04:32:35-07:00","id":1554221,"title":"","content":"I tend to think it's a bad idea. <i>Celestial Chorus Revised</i> tries to differentiate the faith of True Faith from the faith the Choristers use to do their magic, and personally I think it falls flat. Like, you end up with the Chorus just being delusional, mistaking their own will for the will of the One. Now, I'm not saying that that's wrong, but I think it's a mistake to just flatly state it like that. <br /> <br /> I would say, I like the version <i>Revised</i> uses. Like, the mechanics are bit iffy, but the idea is much more interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-07T08:12:37-07:00","id":1554223,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I'm not a fan either.<br /> <br /> It's certainly RAW that Mages can have it. Metaphisically, I'm ok with the justification that Mages can keep this Numina (instead of it &quot;blending&quot; with Magick like every other Numina) because it's power that comes from an external source. <hr /><br /> But I feel like TF on Mage it's a legacy from Vampire that we shouldn't have had. And it was also bad for the identity of TF itself .<br /> <br /> TF was introduced on Vampire as a mechanic to have the classic interaction of a priest expelling the unholly and such, and it works because the setting it's both strongly christian, and focused arround what happens to vampires (so other &quot;manifestations&quot; of the divine, such as, say, having visions offering revelation, don't need to be ruled - they're ST fiat). If it's so costly for what it does, it's because it was such an extreme path for a vampire to take - again, for the context I think that works.<br /> <br /> But then you go to Mage and it sort of *had* to be opened to something more universal that can work on every faith. Then it was a matter of time until it ends being open to any &quot;beliefs&quot;, such as the polemical &quot;faith in capitalism&quot; (the joke on Syndicate agent expelling a vampire with a credit card :P ). The list of powers of TF doesn't always reflect very well with how other belief systems would combat evil, much less how a &quot;holly person&quot; (of sorts) would manifest faith.<br /> <br /> It's cost it's abusive for something a &quot;faith person&quot; would be expected to have, and the only way to rationalize this it's to be cynical and say faith Mages are rarely &quot;true believers&quot;. Which it's WoD levels of edginess, allright, but doesn't mesh super well with the idea of mages trully believing in their Focus.<br /> <br /> It fells like and odd legacy system to me. Too costly for what it gives, and failing to represent what it's supposed to represent, that keeps on getting reprinted just because it was always there, and we do need a system for the effects of &quot;faith&quot;, and this it's a familiar answer to the question (even if it ain't a particularly good answer).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-07-2025, 11:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-09T21:28:46-07:00","id":1554285,"title":"","content":"I tend to think of True Faith as an inversion of Awakened Magick: what they have in common is that both are fundamentally about the power of belief; but where Awakened Magick is about that belief shaping reality according to the mage's will (which tends to foster a certain hubris that the game is known for), True Faith demands a fundamental <i>humility: </i>&quot;not my will; but thine be done.&quot; Because the key feature of True Faith miracle working is that the Faithful aren't the ones working the miracle; they're servants of a higher power that has a will of its own. <br /> <br /> With that said: in game terms, I prefer to treat the Faithful as a minor variation on the Awakened, featuring a more empowered Avatar. As written, all Mage says about the Avatar is that it's that thing that lets the mage Willwork: no Avatar, no Magick. The book doesn't nail down precisely what the Avatar <i>is, </i>though. And for the Faithful, I choose to interpret the Avatar as the conduit connecting the Faithful to the higher power that he or she serves. And with that understanding of what a faith-based Avatar is, I interpret miracles as Wild Talent; specifically, where a Willworking involves the mage using the Avatar to impose the mage's will on reality, a miracle working involves the Avatar using the Faithful's willingness to serve to impose the higher power's will on reality. As a Wild Talent, the Faithful doesn't need Spheres to work a miracle; he technically doesn't even need Arete. What he needs is what the True Faith Merit represents: to be someone who has established and maintains a harmony with the will of a higher power. <br /> <br /> That is: in game terms, True Faith enables Wild Talent. And aside from Marauderhood, it's the <i>only </i>way to reliably access Wild Talent \u2014 for some definition of &quot;reliably&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-12T14:36:40-07:00","id":1554322,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure if it's a problem, but I'm having some doubts.Ofc one could have some faith in the work of a &quot;mysterius entity&quot; that could help sometimes and that's not a problem in RAW, but then again I think I need to think after all the points made here...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:14:05-07:00","id":1554376,"title":"","content":"To be honest I feel that True Faith should be something available only to the otherwise-powerless: if you already have access to Awesome Supernatural Powers (Or are currently<br /> developing same) then True Faith is just a party trick.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if you\u2019re a mere mortal striving against a whole World of Darkness then True Faith is not only your greatest weapon but a rare (and possible lonely) comfort.<br /> <br /> THAT is what True Faith should be, in my opinion, especially on a storytelling level - something that gives the Supernatural pause, not least because they can rationalise it, but never quite explain it as a mere extension of their own powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-15T11:06:56-07:00","id":1554379,"title":"","content":"Well, but then it'd become something that would only be really suitable for an NPC. I don't see a problem in having other powers and having True Faith. I just have to agree that in terms of atmosphere and the themes of every splat,it certainly fits much better into Vampire and definitely not very well with Mage. But, in general, I tend to agree with the RAW as it's not really a problem to have TF as well as other powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-20T21:14:46-07:00","id":1554535,"title":"","content":"As others have said, nothing strictly prevents it. But I do think how TF and Magick can coexist in one person would say alot about the nature of reality in your game. You can take True Faith on face value, as a form of Maurauderhood, as tapping into dominant consensus, as some special paradoxalike...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-22T17:29:34-07:00","id":1554574,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1554285#post1554285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That is: in game terms, True Faith enables Wild Talent. And aside from Marauderhood, it's the <i>only </i>way to reliably access Wild Talent \u2014 for some definition of &quot;reliably&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds quite interesting. Could you expound more about how that actually plays out in games?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26432,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","user":"JagneStormskull","date":".  Correcting a word. 10-29-2025, 03:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T11:44:57-07:00","id":1554666,"title":"","content":"Largely by Storyteller fiat. But if the Storyteller thinks of the mage's Avatar as a character, kind of like a Mentor, with a will, an agenda, and a personality, you'll have a good idea as to when and how the Avatar will perform a miracle on the character's behalf.<br /> <br /> Or, put another way: handle it in narrative terms rather than system terms. Is the mage properly devoting him/herself to his/her lord, to the point where s/he's willing to put him/herself in trouble in His behalf? Is s/he regularly seeking guidance through prayer in order to understand what His will is?(See the Avatar Background for more on when the higher power provides said guidance.) Make the Arete rolls as normal for the Wild Talent, but interpret a failed roll not as the higher power being unable to help, but rather as the higher power testing the mage's faith; and interpret Paradox Backlashes through the lens of the higher power being angry with the mage, but not (usually) so angry as to impose the Faithless Flaw. Fudge the results in those cases where it wouldn't make sense in terms of the story's narrative, just as you would for <i>any </i>Storyteller roll that has the potential to derail the game.<br /> <br /> That sort of thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 12-12-2025, 05:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-06T17:31:25-08:00","id":1556273,"title":"","content":"Just saw that merit in the Book of Shadows; a mage with True-Faith can be scary; they have god-like powers with conviction they are righteous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-08T14:52:54-08:00","id":1557384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith#post1554198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I'd love to hear some opinions regarding this question posed to me in one of my games. What do you think of the idea of a religious mage that also has True Faith besides his Magick?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I like to think that True Faith is just the sorcerous equivalent to being a Celestial Chorister.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-08T17:15:35-08:00","id":1557391,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557384#post1557384\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I like to think that True Faith is just the sorcerous equivalent to being a Celestial Chorister.</div> </div> </div> </div>That depends on what you have <b>TRUE FAITH </b>in. If your faith isn't in a monotheistic god, or something that can be interpreted as a monotheistic god, then you aren't a Chorister. If a Sorcerer had truth faith in the Olympian gods as a community of Gods, then that's <b>TRUE FAITH </b>without monotheism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-08T20:36:53-08:00","id":1557396,"title":"","content":"Both of those are fairly reductive takes on what makes for a religious person; though not entirely inappropriate with the elder members of the Chorus and even more so those that might remember the pre-Traditions eras.<br /> <br /> Monotheism isn't as clear cut of a concept as that. If you take a strict monotheist definition-stance, then the Chorus isn't actually monotheist even if it ultimately suborns all deities to The One (having True Faith in Zeus isn't any different from having True Faith in Jesus as far as the Chorus is truly constructed). If you take a looser definition where henoism, monolartry, and gnostic systems fall under a wider monotheistic umbrella then it doesn't really matter.<br /> <br /> The bigger reason why True Faith doesn't work as &quot;sorcerers for the Chorus&quot; is that True Faith is far too narrow a concept for all religious mystic practices that non-Awakened magic users associated with the Chorus might practice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-08T21:34:50-08:00","id":1557397,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557391#post1557391\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That depends on what you have <b>TRUE FAITH </b>in. If your faith isn't in a monotheistic god, or something that can be interpreted as a monotheistic god, then you aren't a Chorister. If a Sorcerer had truth faith in the Olympian gods as a community of Gods, then that's <b>TRUE FAITH </b>without monotheism.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, I should clarify. I meant the sorcerous equivalent in the sense that it is faith based magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:07:04-08:00","id":1557404,"title":"","content":"Right; but the devil is in the details. What does it <i>mean</i> for magic to be faith-based? <br /> <br /> As I mentioned in my earlier posts, I think the defining quality is that faith-based magic is performed in service to a higher power that is in some way personified and makes demands of the magic user's conduct. That's why you can't have, say, True Faith in &quot;the Scientific Method&quot; or anything else that's impersonal; and &quot;True Faith&quot; in an entity that simply doesn't care what the &quot;Faithful&quot; does undermines the single biggest downside of True Faith: that you can lose everything if you disappoint said entity. The nature of that entity isn't as important; as Astromancer points out, it doesn't even heaved to be monotheistic \u2014 though I suspect that someone with True Faith in &quot;the gods of Olympus&quot; would either not work (if the character is free to decide at any moment exactly which god they're serving, effectively treating the set as something that you can't really disappoint) or would be incredibly punishing (if the character <i>doesn't </i>get to choose who is going to be making demands). This, as opposed to having True Faith in <i>one particular </i>Olympian such as Zeus or Ares, where there will be demands on you but at least some consistency in terms of what those demands will be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:25:00-08:00","id":1557405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557404#post1557404\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">though I suspect that someone with True Faith in &quot;the gods of Olympus&quot; would either not work (if the character is free to decide at any moment exactly which god they're serving, effectively treating the set as something that you can't really disappoint) or would be incredibly punishing (if the character <i>doesn't </i>get to choose who is going to be making demands).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think that might be a touch inaccurate. The different gods of Olympus fulfilled different roles. You wouldn't pray to the God of War to grow your crops, because that's not what his portfolio entails. You don't change which deity you're praying to because it's convenient, you switch them based on which deity applies to the situation at that current moment.<br /> <br /> Want to slaughter your enemies by the score, and aren't squeamish about your methods? Pray to Ares.<br /> <br /> Want to secure a victory that won't result in high amounts of collateral damage? Pray to Athena.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-09T03:39:03-08:00","id":1557412,"title":"","content":"A good example of how True Faith doesn't really work as a concept here. It should be entirely possible to deeply offend one god of the Olympus, yet have the favor of another. It's a big thing on mitology.<br /> <br /> Now, having Magic be the same as Faith (for some mages/sorcerers) doesn't have such problem. You could easily justify a penalty on a set of effects or paths, while having normal access to others. I think that's the better way of doing it on Mage, but that's not using True Faith RAW","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-09T11:15:32-08:00","id":1557422,"title":"","content":"A Buddhist can have faith in Buddha's teachings. Buddhist <b>TRUE FAITH </b>wouldn't be faith in a personal deity but in the nature of reality as explained by the Buddha and his followers. This wildly contradicts several things others have said on this thread. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-10T20:38:17-08:00","id":1557461,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557405#post1557405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I think that might be a touch inaccurate. The different gods of Olympus fulfilled different roles. You wouldn't pray to the God of War to grow your crops, because that's not what his portfolio entails. You don't change which deity you're praying to because it's convenient, you switch them based on which deity applies to the situation at that current moment..</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably a nitpick, but didn't the Roman counterpart of Ares, Mars, also have a role in agriculture?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-11T00:55:45-08:00","id":1557462,"title":"","content":"Ares and Mars (who yes, is also an agricultural deity and was generally far better liked by the Romans than Ares was by the Greeks) are two of the more disparate deities between the Greek and Roman pantheons. While there's enough overlap between the beliefs that they're frequently conflated, there are also a number of fairly significant differences (including fairly major entities that exist in one and not the other). Though it's hard to say how much those differences are truly representative across time and history since most of our most comprehensive sources tend to be from later periods so we can't entirely reproduce variations over the entire breadth of Greek religious history.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons Athena tended to be more popular over Ares is that she could be said to be a goddess of warfare, while Ares could be said to be the god of the battlefield. Ares being depicted as being accompanied in battle by Eris, the goddess of chaos, and his sons Phobos and Deimos only heightens the perception of Ares as personifying the horrific sides of combat. The brutal reality of fighting to Athena's higher order strategies.<br /> <br /> Mars was generally treated far more neutrally (and rarely with such terror inspiring company), and very much reflected the Roman dedication to victory via logistics and infrastructure. Roman soldiers were trained to be combat engineers, road builders, and were even expected to start farms to support distant fort-towns. Mars having a stronger duality of both the violence of war and the need to make sure soldiers are fed let him be far more popular. This is also reflected in Minerva, Athena's counterpart, having a different role as well. While still a war goddess in her own right, she blessed the rulers (Senate or Empire) who directed the war from her temple in Rome (privileged to sit on the Capitoline Hill with only Jupiter and Juno's temples) rather than get involved with more hands on affairs.<br /> <br /> ---------------<br /> <br /> Though the way the Greco-Roman deities reflected the different values of their peoples creates an interesting interaction with the concept of True Faith and some of the ancient Greek philosophies. The nature of their gods was intensely debated among the Greeks, and a prominent formulation was that each of them (or each of the original six Olympians, or the later twelve...) represented a virtue to live up to. Sort of like the Ten Commandments or the Noble Eightfold Path... but with personalities. From that theological perspective True Faith in the pantheon as a whole is a more coherent structure as such an adherent would be less concerned with the idea of interacting with the gods on a person-to-person level, and more concerned with working to &quot;be in the favor&quot; of each of them by living in accordance with the virtues in question. Show the bravery of Ares amidst stark conflict would be more important than trying to juggle cultic rituals between so many deities as one person.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-12T17:37:39-08:00","id":1557528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557404#post1557404\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right; but the devil is in the details. What does it <i>mean</i> for magic to be faith-based?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The way I would see it, it's magic that draws power from the strength of your belief in it.<br /> <br /> Much like how sorcery is pretty much just Hermeticism with fewer perks.<br /> <br /> You don't need to be a Hermetic to practice sorcery, but sorcerers are effectively using the same methods in their spell casting.<br /> <br /> Likewise, you don't need to believe in The One to use True Faith, but having faith in the power of something uses the same method as the Choristers.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-12T18:27:25-08:00","id":1557530,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GreatHornedRatkin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557528#post1557528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Much like how sorcery is pretty much just Hermeticism with fewer perks.<br /> <br /> You don't need to be a Hermetic to practice sorcery, but sorcerers are effectively using the same methods in their spell casting.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That's the same impression I got. Follow the instructions properly, and you produce the desired result for your spells. The main difference being Hermetics are free to invent new spells, while Sorcerers can not.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-12T20:16:09-08:00","id":1557534,"title":"","content":"I'm really not sure where you two are getting these things from.<br /> <br /> Sorcerer in the WoD is a catch-all for countless forms of human systems. Technocrats of hyper-tech versions of Sorcery Paths. The only explicitly hermetic forms of Sorcery are either taught by the Order of Hermes, or constructed from the same western mystic traditions. A Shinto shrine keeper that uses Sorcery Paths to deal directly with the Kami is not doing hermetic sorcery even if the mechanics are effectively the same as what hermetic sorcerers use to deal with umbrood.<br /> <br /> Truth Faith and being a Chorister simply aren't the same thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-12T22:49:48-08:00","id":1557536,"title":"","content":"According to M20 Sorcerer, there are Sorcerers who Practice Faith.<br /> <br /> Also: Magic that draws its power from your belief in it is called Awakened Magick. In order to differentiate True Faith from Awakened Magick, you need more of a definition than that. That's why, from my first post here, I've been insisting that True Faith involves the belief involving some sort of moral code that you must adhere to, or face consequences. When True Faith was first introduced in Vampire, the Humanity trait provided that moral code; adapting True Faith to other gamelines removes the game system for the moral code, but doesn't remove the need to adhere to one.<br /> <br /> In short, &quot;faith-based&quot; isn't empowered by belief in the magic; it's empowered by belief in the morals. To the Faithful, there's Right and Wrong, and you should do what's Right and avoid what's Wrong no matter what it costs you.<br /> <br /> True Faith isn't about Magic; it's about Religion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":".  Magic vs. Religion. 01-13-2026, 02:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-13T07:33:40-08:00","id":1557546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557534#post1557534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm really not sure where you two are getting these things from.</div> </div> </div> </div>When I hear about how formulaic Sorcery is, my mind just defaults to imagining them as performing classical wizard stuff. I suppose you're right in that any of the traditions could teach Sorcerers to mimic their methods, but Hermeticism is the one that immediately springs to mind for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-13T11:40:13-08:00","id":1557552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557536#post1557536\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Magic that draws its power from your belief in it is called Awakened Magick. In order to differentiate True Faith from Awakened Magick, you need more of a definition than that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh... a lot of the setting would disagree with your own definitions. The only definition we need of True Faith is the one in the book already. Same with Awakened Magic. These are game terms so that we know there's specific things that exist in their specific way in the WoD. While their inspirations and reasons for being adapted to the WoD are important, so is remembering that they have been adapted to the WoD. Getting too caught up in definitions from outside the WoD only makes things too confusing.<br /> <br /> In the WoD, True Faith is about belief, not religion. The big difference between True Faith and Awakened Magic is that True Faith is a Numina. It's magic of the baseline WoD and follows the occult rules of the setting; which happen to include requiring strong belief/faith to be able to use it. It doesn't require religion, just like technically Awakened Magic doesn't require instruments... it's just a lot easier going down well worn paths than trying to make it all up on your own. Truth Faith, like M20 core's discussion on paradigms, is going to be subject to practicality bias. Religions (good luck defining what that even means) provide a preexisting framework for faith to endure a world that doesn't conform to faith. The benefits of being a member of a religion - even IRL - are pretty nice even if there's zero objective way to prove your faith in the religion's doctrines is logically sound. Religions that have been around for thousands of years have endured a lot of changes in how people perceive the world and judge institutions. Trying to have True Faith in being a Jedi, or The Power of Friendship, isn't impossible. It's just a lot harder because it's a more brittle belief structure without generations of adaptations and apologetics to help you get past all the surface level problems of putting so much personal stakes on such matters.<br /> <br /> So most True Faith characters are going to come from established religions. Then again, so do most Awakened Magic paradigms (even ones that have grown more secular over time). Even the Etherites can't completely divorce themselves from some faith aspects to their philosophy in the form of their founding mythology. The majority of the Traditions, Crafts, and Disparates have been built on faith traditions. And it makes sense for the same reason True Faith is going to crop up more when there's religion around.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GreatHornedRatkin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557546#post1557546\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I hear about how formulaic Sorcery is, my mind just defaults to imagining them as performing classical wizard stuff. I suppose you're right in that any of the traditions could teach Sorcerers to mimic their methods, but Hermeticism is the one that immediately springs to mind for me.</div> </div> </div> </div>OK, but we're talking about a game, where there are specific definitions of a lot of things. If you're not reading how sorcery is defined in the WoD books, it's going to be hard to talk about how to apply it to the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-13T15:22:15-08:00","id":1557562,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The big difference between True Faith and Awakened Magic is that True Faith is a Numina.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay; but what does that <i>mean? </i>Hedge Paths are a Numina, too; and so are Psychic Phenomena. Saying &quot;it's a Numina&quot; only says that it's supernatural and that mortals can access it. It doesn't say anything about its nature. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Religions (good luck defining what that even means)</div> </div> </div> </div>I wasn't meaning religion in a formal sense; but rather in the sense of the following contrast:<br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The main difference between magic and religion lies in their approach to the supernatural: religion involves faith, worship, and seeking a relationship with divine powers (supplication), while magic attempts to control or manipulate those powers for specific, often personal, outcomes (coercion). Religion emphasizes obedience, community, and moral codes, focusing on a broader worldview, whereas magic is more instrumental, technical, and focused on immediate, tangible results for the practitioner, often through secret or private rituals.<br /> <br /> <b>Religion</b><ul><li><b>Goal:</b> To understand the universe, find purpose, and foster a connection with the divine.</li> <li><b>Method:</b> Worship, prayer, devotion, faith, and adherence to moral codes.</li> <li><b>Relationship:</b> Seeks to please or propitiate higher powers (submission).</li> <li><b>Community:</b> Communal, building shared beliefs and institutions (churches).</li> <li><b>Scope:</b> Often provides a complete worldview and long-term spiritual goals (afterlife).</li> </ul><b>Magic</b><ul><li><b>Goal</b>: To achieve specific, immediate, and practical results (e.g., wealth, love, healing).</li> <li><b>Method</b>: Technical acts, incantations, charms, and manipulating natural/supernatural forces.</li> <li><b>Relationship</b>: Seeks to control or compel supernatural forces (domination).</li> <li><b>Community</b>: Often private, individualistic, or with temporary ties to practitioners.</li> <li><b>Scope</b>: Focuses on instrumental action and immediate, measurable effects.</li> </ul><b>Key Distinction: Control vs. Faith</b><ul><li><b>Magic:</b> &quot;I know the laws, so I can make the power do my bidding&quot;.</li> <li><b>Religion:</b> &quot;I have faith in the power, so I ask it to help me&quot;.</li> </ul>While distinct in theory, these practices often overlap, with some viewing magic as an archaic form of religion or a subset of it, and different cultures blurring these lines significantly.</div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trying to have True Faith in being a Jedi, or The Power of Friendship, isn't impossible. It's just a lot harder because it's a more brittle belief structure without generations of adaptations and apologetics to help you get past all the surface level problems of putting so much personal stakes on such matters.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am <i>not</i> saying that True Faith needs to conform to the views of an established, real-world religion. But at the same time, I don't see how one can reasonably argue that True Faith <i>isn't</i> religious. Different Faithful may disagree on what &quot;the divine&quot; is that they're trying to foster a connection with; but every last one has a notion of some higher authority that is to be respected and obeyed. <br /> <br /> In particular, every version of True Faith that I've found in a World of Darkness book includes an element in it to the effect of there being some sort of moral code that the Faithful must adhere to or face consequences; usually, the consequences are phrased as &quot;you lose your True Faith&quot;. The reason why True Faith is considered to be rare in the World of Darkness is because it's so easy to lose it. <br /> <br /> That's why I distinguished &quot;belief in the magic&quot; vs. &quot;belief in the morals&quot;: there's often a tendency when talking about True Faith to focus on what it does for you while neglecting the part about what it demands from you. While &quot;belief in the morals&quot; isn't <i>precisely</i> correct, it's closer to the truth than &quot;belief in the magic&quot; is, in that it acknowledges that True Faith involves some sort of worship and obedience of <i>something</i> greater than oneself, whereas &quot;belief in magic&quot; comes across as an acknowledgement of magic's existence. <br /> <br /> With that said, I will agree that the line between magic and religion is fuzzy. I've said as much from my very first post in this topic, which is why I pointed out that it's entirely reasonable for the Storyteller to handle True Faith as Awakened Magick Focused through the Practice of Faith. This shouldn't be controversial; the revised <b>Celestial Chorus Tradition Book</b> says as much. But then there's the Faithless Flaw mentioned in <b>Book of Secrets</b> that Practitioners of Faith are vulnerable to but other mages aren't. Which brings me back to &quot;religion demands worship and obedience; magic doesn't.&quot;<br /> <br /> And this goes for the True Faith Merit, too: if your belief system doesn't include some sort of ethical framework that constrains your behavior, it isn't True Faith. \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-13T17:20:22-08:00","id":1557567,"title":"","content":"Ultimately though, you're just arguing from your own definitions. How can anyone argue if you're setting the terms with a definition that presupposes your position is correct? You're not defining &quot;religion&quot; and &quot;magic&quot; in a fashion that tries to describe what those are, you're defining them to get to the conclusion you want to get to; so of course your conclusion seems the best.<br /> <br /> A crucial issue is that religion is a belief system, while magic is an action based on a type of belief system. Religions are something you believe in, magic is something you do. When you start to say what acting in accordance with a religion means, or what belief systems result in people performing the act of magic, the line doesn't just get blurry, it disappears completely. You can talk all you want about how people don't focus on what True Faith demands of the faithful... but that's neglecting the demands of Paradigm in Mage just as much for how Awakened Magic works.<br /> <br /> It's really simple to try to say philosophy and computer programming as distinct and separate things, but it's making the same category error where the distinctions all come down to looking at different parts of the whole. Computer programming is doing something based on the principles of symbolic logic that derive from philosophy and the formulation of logical thought systems. They're not the same, in a sense, but &quot;Christianity,&quot; and &quot;having a baptism,&quot; aren't the same either in functionally identical fashion; and thus are parts of the same whole.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-13T18:42:10-08:00","id":1557571,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557567#post1557567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ultimately though, you're just arguing from your own definitions. How can anyone argue if you're setting the terms with a definition that presupposes your position is correct? You're not defining &quot;religion&quot; and &quot;magic&quot; in a fashion that tries to describe what those are, you're defining them to get to the conclusion you want to get to; so of course your conclusion seems the best.</div> </div> </div> </div> I got those definitions from Google; I didn't make them up myself. There are several aspects of them that I wouldn't have thought of myself, such as the communal nature of religion, and a few elements in those definitions that I'm not comfortable with. So no, that's not me making up definitions to get the conclusion I want.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557567#post1557567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A crucial issue is that religion is a belief system, while magic is an action based on a type of belief system. Religions are something you believe in, magic is something you do.</div> </div> </div> </div>A religion is a type of belief system; there are arguably belief systems that aren't religions. In particular, when discussing Faith (be it the True Faith Merit or the Practice of Faith), religion involves a god or gods of some sort; and atheistic belief systems aren't religious. See below.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557567#post1557567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When you start to say what acting in accordance with a religion means, or what belief systems result in people performing the act of magic, the line doesn't just get blurry, it disappears completely. You can talk all you want about how people don't focus on what True Faith demands of the faithful... but that's neglecting the demands of Paradigm in Mage just as much for how Awakened Magic works.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have never denied that Paradigms can make similar demands. I pointed out the example of Awakened Mages who Practice Faith, remember? But codes of morality aren't inherent to Awakened Magick the way they are to True Faith. There's a reason the Faithless Flaw exists, and why said Flaw starts out with:<br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Magick, according to your beliefs, does not come from you \u2014 it comes from your god, and you've broken faith with him.</div><br /> Also:<br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Although a character with this Flaw may pursue any spiritual focus, his paradigm and practice must be oriented toward religious devotion. ... Obviously, this Flaw means nothing to an atheist, or to someone with nebulously casual beliefs. Only a mage who believes deeply in some greater power can believe that she has so much to lose from a sudden lack of faith.</div><br /> (Both Book of Secrets p.93.)<br /> <br /> Mages may or may not have a belief in a higher power; those that do are vulnerable to the Faithless Flaw, while those who don't, aren't. That's not my idea; that's published M20 material. The only thing I add to it is the notion that mages who are vulnerable to the Faithless Flaw might also arguably have more reliable access to Wild Talent, in the form of the higher power they believe in acting through them.<br /> <br /> By contrast, here's what M20 says about True Faith:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Gamewise, your character has one dot of True Faith \u2014 a potent force, in the World of Darkness, that reflects devotion to a god or pantheon. \u2026<br /> <br /> Faith is no simple thing. It must be lived as well as recognized. A person with True Faith strives to be an exemplar of her creed, suffers crises of conviction, and might find herself losing the Faith entirely. \u2026 Deeds in accord with that Faith strengthen or restore it, whereas acts opposed to the creed weaken this Merit.</div><br /> (M20, pp.644\u2013646.)<br /> <br /> There are no exceptions: if you have True Faith, you must live your creed or lose your Faith.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":".  Formatting error 01-13-2026, 09:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T03:12:37-08:00","id":1557584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557571#post1557571\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I got those definitions from Google; I didn't make them up myself.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that's a very bad place to take serious definitions from, as it will poorly summarize an extremely complex academic discussion, ignore the several conflicts between the views that are being mixed in this summary as if they were the same thing, all that in the best days, when it isn't hallucinating shit. By <i>got from Google</i> I bet you mean you got from Gemini (the AI doing those summaries), and both as someone who studied those concepts academically and as someone who studies LLMs now, I have to say that trusting Gemini/Google is a very problematic move.<br /> <br /> I know this distinction between Faith and Magic you described, and it is terribly out of context and severely butchered by conflations. More to the point, though, it isn't meant to apply to anything resembling what we're talking about even in this form. Long story short, this is about the distinction between what you believe in and how you apply this belief in your life, and <i>by definition</i> it's talking about two sides of the same system every time. It's basically saying that going to church is the religion side, praying to get a job is the magic side of this religion. There's a little more to it in the form of the work of Versnel, but that's the terribly out of context part (Wikipedia cites his definition for didactic purposes, but isn't done very well and ends up taking far more focus than it should given the very limited context in which it was used and what Versnel was really writing about).<br /> <br /> So it actually isn't a distinction between True Faith and Awakened Magic, because both are about believing in something and then deriving power from this belief. Paradigm and Faith are both &quot;religion&quot;, spell effects and True Faith effects are both magic.<br /> <br /> There is a difference between them, but it isn't anything about what you believe in or how you believe in it. Instead, the difference is literally in the very nature of the person: Awakened Magic comes from someone capable at forcing reality to conform to their belief, while True Faith is an element of reality already. And that's also why Heavy Arms' defining it simply as &quot;Numina&quot; is precisely on point. True Faith is, like every Numina, a sort of power that normal people can have under the natural rules of the World of Darkness without ceasing to be normal people as far as it goes.<br /> <br /> Does True Faith requires morality? Arguably yes, but so does most Paradigms (arguably all of them, in a sense), it's just that Mages that change their minds also change their Magic, while normal people don't get this benefit. Anything else we say about them is either semantics or speculation.<br /> <br /> You shouldn't be so fast at stipulating the presence of gods as a defining trait, either. What even is a god? Many forms of Buddhism, Spiritism, some Shinto, Religious Taoism and Confucianism, are <i><b>exactly</b></i> deity-less religions. And while WoD's Order of Hermes is seriously agnostic/atheistic as it is based on Telema, most &quot;hermetic&quot; mysticism and magical practices through history is heavily based on the assumption that gods and their servants exist, including Christian hermeticism through the last two thousand years of European occultism. The very distinction between magic and religion <i><b>as we understand it today</b></i> only started to take form in the 18th century and really took it by the late 19th century, early 20th, only to be criticized by Anthropology already in the 30's.<br /> <br /> When we talk about True Faith and Awakened Magic in the context of the game, we're talking about very specific mechanics, and the metaphysics that fuel those mechanics pertain to the game, not to strict definitions on morality or the distinction between religion and magic. Awakened Magic comes from an Awakened person, the end. The nature of Awakening is definitely relevant, but isn't defined as exploring it and finding out is one of the elements of the game, yet we can say for <i>certain</i> that they're not normal people. True Faith comes from a perfectly normal person, with a quite <i>abnormal</i> level of trust in a given belief, that for unknown reasons resonate with the background supernatural forces of the universe, and lives accordingly, the end. Any further explanation is beyond the scope of the game except as a possible mystery to investigate.<br /> <br /> Not to say, as Heavy Arms already said, that it is a very bad rule in which it gives a set of requirements and effects meant to cover what it means to be faithful in any &quot;religion&quot;, but it's actually only really fitting for a sample of Christian denominations, going from &quot;kinda wonky&quot; to &quot;completely misses the point&quot; when you go outside of this narrow set of religions. And even within it, it's still only fitting for some stories, mostly Vampire stories and a bit of exorcism stories, plus random gamefied effects tackled on to complete the power set.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T05:07:50-08:00","id":1557589,"title":"","content":"Speaking of True Faith, do you guys think a Tulpa would qualify as part of that category? Maybe acing in the role of some sort of conjured spirit?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T07:34:00-08:00","id":1557599,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I got those definitions from Google; I didn't make them up myself. There are several aspects of them that I wouldn't have thought of myself, such as the communal nature of religion, and a few elements in those definitions that I'm not comfortable with. So no, that's not me making up definitions to get the conclusion I want.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're awfull. I'm don't want to debate, but I fell like I have to at least take the time to jab a litte:<br /> <br /> &quot;the communal nature of religion&quot;. What about Hermits that leave their communities to find God?<br /> <br /> &quot;Religion: To understand the universe, find purpose, and foster a connection with the divine. Magic: To achieve specific, immediate, and practical results (e.g., wealth, love, healing).&quot;<br /> <br /> So, praying for someone to heal it's magick as it's hoping for a practical result.<br /> <br /> Being an alchemist that tries to refine himself to become closer with the divine it's Religion because it's a pracrtice that encourages understanding the universe and foster a connection with the divine<br /> <br /> Don't trust the Abominable Intelligence","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T09:07:37-08:00","id":1557603,"title":"","content":"To be fair, <i>I </i>didn't say that True Faith requires a god or pantheon; <b><i>M20</i></b> did. Also, I never said that the definition I gave is the currently accepted one; only that I didn't make it up, contrary to Heavy Arms' claim, and that that definition <i>does </i>appear to be closer to the one that <b>M20</b> is using.<br /> <br /> Likewise, <b>Book of Secrets</b> doesn't appear to be using the religion/magic distinction that you're referring to in its writeup of Faithless: whether or not it's technically accurate, it's clearly assuming a distinction between religion and magic that's more like what I outlined. Because if it wasn't \u2014 if, in effect, religion is whatever your Paradigm is \u2014 it would be meaningless to say that the Faithless Flaw only comes into play when the mage's paradigm and practice is religious, like saying &quot;this only applies if water is wet&quot;. And it explicitly calls out atheists as people who aren't subject to the Flaw, and speaks of needing a deep belief in a higher power before the Flaw can apply. It <i>doesn't</i> say that the gods or pantheons actually need to exist; only that the mage has to believe in them.<br /> <br /> And both of these build on <b>MRev</b>'s definitions of magic and True Faith, as described in the Revised <b>Celestial Chorus Tradition Book</b>:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The crux of a great divide \u2026 lies in the nature of True Faith versus magic. A Chorister performs miracles through the power of will, using tools that consecrate or create a bond with divinity. The Faithful perform miracles by becoming channels of some greater power. The lines do blur. A devout Chorister sees her will as simply an extension of the One. The major difference is that True Faith relies on the intervention of some outside agency, even if the faith itself springs from within. A Chorister could, conversely, be ultimately faithless and yet still work magic. Such a mage would be a sad creature indeed, since she would be hobbled by her belief that she can\u2019t do magic until she regains her faith.<br /> <br /> In game terms, both True Faith and magic allow for some phenomenal miracles, but True Faith is the less structured of the two systems. How, then, do you justify having both in play? What do you do if you have a character with both? Can a Chorister serve a greater power through True Faith and yet also have personal power?</div> <br /> And it then offers the following options:<ul><li><b>True Faith and magic co-exist</b>: The usual World of Darkness style. True Faith is a channeling of some greater power through profound personal surrender. Magic is a channeling of personal power through personal will.</li> <li><b>True Faith and magic co-exist, but not in the</b><b>same person</b>: You could argue that a Chorister cannot have True Faith because she imposes her will on Creation, while the Faithful instead are nothing more than agents of Creation who subsume their will in the Divine. In this model, no Chorister has True Faith. They have Awakened to personal power, and their service to the One is always a matter of using their own devices. The Faithful, in the end, are vessels who are directed where they must be.</li> <li><b>True Faith <i>is</i> Awakened magic</b>: The Faithful are just Orphans! That is, if you decide to use this option. Doing so solves some neat little loopholes with metaphysics and makes the game a little more internally contiguous. On the other hand, this option also eliminates a wonderful little stumper \u2014 the real world doesn\u2019t always make perfect sense, and neither does the game world. In this model, the Faithful just have some sort of proto-Awakened magic that allows them to perform limited feats. A faith healer, for instance, is really an Orphan with Life magic.</li> <li><b>There is no True Faith</b>: God is dead, and so are His servants in this variant, so nobody has True Faith. Perhaps humans can\u2019t know the Divine, and the Chorus is doomed. Perhaps Awakened magic is the only route to real human cosmic power. Perhaps the One does exist, but only in a distant form that does not interact with humanity.</li> <li><b>Choristers aren\u2019t mages at all</b>: This option is pretty far-flung, but you can rule that Choristers aren\u2019t really mages. Instead, they are legions of the Faithful, with theurgists (sorcerers of holy magic) backing them up. In this variant, you still have to deal with the metaphysical nit-picking of Faith and magic in the same setting, but suddenly you make the Chorus take a very different direction in mage society. Can you train someone to have True Faith, even though you can\u2019t train an Awakening? What are the limits of True Faith, and how does it compare to magic? What happens if anyone ever figures out that the Septarians are right and that the Traditions really are just heathen wizards?</li> </ul>Note that the first option explicitly describes the dichotomy that I mentioned: Awakened Magick is the channeling of personal power through personal will, and True Faith is channeling a greater power through profound personal surrender. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not to say, as Heavy Arms already said, that it is a very bad rule in which it gives a set of requirements and effects meant to cover what it means to be faithful in any &quot;religion&quot;, but it's actually only really fitting for a sample of Christian denominations, going from &quot;kinda wonky&quot; to &quot;completely misses the point&quot; when you go outside of this narrow set of religions. </div> </div> </div>Also from the <b>Celestial Chorus Tradition Book</b>: <br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Remember, exercises of Faith often follow the established sorts of miracles for the individual\u2019s religion. A Christian might walk on water, turn water to wine or heal the sick. A Buddhist might float in the air, meditate without food for a year or become invisible. A Mithraite might breathe fire or make his skin as impermeable as iron.</div>In other words, True Faith <i>isn't</i> restricted to Christianity and Christian-adjacent religions. (And yes, I'm aware that Buddhism is an agnostic religion.) M20 goes so far as to say that a <i>Nephandus</i> can have True Faith in the monstrous beings that some Nephandi worship. That ranges from directly opposed (for those Nephandi that are also infernalists) to wildly askew (for the Malfeans and K'llasha).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T17:04:21-08:00","id":1557623,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557599#post1557599\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't trust the Abominable Intelligence</div> </div> </div> </div>Trusting them as specialists is generally a bad idea, they're not and should always be treated as fallible in the best cases. Gemini is worse, because it's currently the LLM that hallucinates the most, and while the presence of sources and its confident posture gives a strong sense of authority, it's actually far from the truth. Gemini doesn't always understand what it got on the search, but it's literally made to never admit it failed. ChatGPT has a similar problem, yet it not only is capable of admitting to it when prompted, but will teach you how to prompt to it in a way to minimize the risk, just ask it. If you want to cite your AI Assistant as an unverified expert, you better get DeepSeek or Claude, the ones built with the valuable ability of saying &quot;I don't know&quot;.<br /> <br /> But that said, in this particular case the worse offender wasn't even the AI, it's just too dumb to notice the problem. Wikipedia is generally a good first source, and that means it has a lot of influence on those results from Gemini (as it uses the first entries in the search batch as sources), but this specific article has a number of problems in how it was written. And the biggest one is devoting a big chunk of premium space to Versnel's definition.<br /> <br /> Problem is: Versnel isn't an Anthropologist. He's a historian specialized in ancient Greece that came out with a ludicrous definition outside his ballpark because it was useful for one of his books. It isn't terrible within academia as it was a footnote in his work, but outside academia it ended up being the thing he wrote that people remember the most. Imagine an Astrophysicist with a decent corpus of work on their field, then someday they write somewhere that bloodletting is an effective treatment for epileptic seizures. And somehow this essay on epilepsy becomes widely accepted by people as the most didactic explanation for seizure treatment, ignoring both the life work of the writer and all the actual experts on epilepsy. That's basically what happens because of that Wikipedia article.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557603#post1557603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, <i>I </i>didn't say that True Faith requires a god or pantheon; <b><i>M20</i></b> did. Also, I never said that the definition I gave is the currently accepted one; only that I didn't make it up, contrary to Heavy Arms' claim, and that that definition <i>does </i>appear to be closer to the one that <b>M20</b> is using.</div> </div> </div> </div>M20 is a game book, not a treatise on religion, no matter how much it tries to dive on metaphysical matters. Describing like that for a mostly Christian audience is just simpler, saving on precious word-count for more relevant sections. And the definition of religion I described was literally me explaining the meaning of what you said, not me defending any definition. No definition matters because this is besides the point, trying to turn the wording of the rules into something they're not by focusing on <i>secondary fluff </i>instead of actual information.<br /> <br /> And if taken literally like the way you say it, then the text contradicts itself. I'll repeat, and suggest you research the matter on your own if you don't understand: <b>There are godless religions, including religions described in True Faith</b>. For most Buddhists, not just a minority, the Buddha is an example, not a divinity. And for <i><b>all</b></i> Buddhists, the feats of great Monks come from self-Enlightenment, not from a higher power. Taoism is that in spades, power comes from understanding the Tao. Kardecism works through spirits of the deceased, but does not worship them, they're at most respected. By your definition those religions shouldn't allow True Faith, period.<br /> <br /> Saying that definition comes from <i>somewhere</i> hardly changes anything at all. And it isn't close to the definition used by the books. The books again state that Mages do Magic because they're Mages, and what they do is inherently Magic. They don't shape the world through Will because that's what they consciously chose to do, they shape the world through Will because that's their nature. And the Faithless Flaw is just, if you didn't notice, a version of Sleepwalker. It's not about the Mage being denied by a higher power, it's an effect of the Mage believing they can't do Magic anymore: a being that shape reality, shaping reality to deny themselves. True Faith channels something that exists in the world, with faith being the naturalistic requirement.<br /> <br /> You're arguing semantics and refusing to engage the core point. Magic and True Faith are distinct phenomena because of setting metaphysics and the nature of the splat, not because of definitions of religion, belief, magic and faith. And they exist together simply because True Faith is an artifact of earlier games. You use them together or not as you see fit, but <i><b>thematically</b></i> they're kind of redundant in Mage unless your chronicle is focused on that question.<br /> <br /> I really don't want to discuss a defense of a simplistic game rule based on an understanding of other religions so shallow you don't even know just how ignorant you are on what you're talking about, but it seems I have to. True Faith doesn't give any rule to make this adaptation, it's just a short line saying you can/should, but you're on your own. And that line is just a later addition, as the original rule was just the Cross and Blessed Water Harm Vampires thing. Even if it had other effects, would still miss the point royally because the very notion of performing miracles through faith in a god is a Christian thing. It isn't even exactly an Abrahamic thing, it's Christian. Similar, but not identical notions exist elsewhere, but very distinct notions also do. Most religions don't give a fuck about faith, by the way, not in the sense Christians do.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T17:22:08-08:00","id":1557624,"title":"","content":"Sometimes, it may be better to NOT have a rope than have a slippery one","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T20:57:36-08:00","id":1557633,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> M20 is a game book, not a treatise on religion, </div> </div> </div>Yes; and we're discussing the game, not a treatise on religion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-14T22:03:16-08:00","id":1557637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1554198-mage-with-true-faith?p=1557623#post1557623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I really don't want to discuss a defense of a simplistic game rule based on an understanding of other religions so shallow you don't even know just how ignorant you are on what you're talking about, but it seems I have to.</div> </div> </div> </div><b><span style=\"color:#c0392b\">This breaks rule 1 and 3. Don't do it again.</span></b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/They<br /> Moderator<br /> Line Developer: <b><a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4261/Onyx-Path-Publishing/subcategory/8329_24824/Scarred-Lands-new\" target=\"_blank\">Scarred Lands</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25411-travislegge","name":"TravisLegge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25411_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}